# Line-ending policy for this repo.
#
# Without this file, line endings depend on each machine's core.autocrlf,
# which produced whole-file CRLF<->LF diffs and "LF will be replaced by CRLF"
# warnings on commit. These rules make it deterministic: every text file is
# stored AND checked out as LF, regardless of platform or git config. Git
# normalizes on commit, so even a working tree that holds CRLF commits clean.

# Default: detect text vs binary, store + check out text as LF.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows shell scripts genuinely require CRLF.
*.bat   text eol=crlf
*.cmd   text eol=crlf

# Generated location catalogs — 34,875 French communes, one entry per line.
# Never hand-edited (scripts/generate-location-catalogs.mjs rewrites them from
# the official INSEE/Tilastokeskus releases), so collapse them in review rather
# than burying a PR under an annual refresh diff.
src/lib/locations/catalog/*.json linguist-generated=true

# Binary assets — never convert.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.webp  binary
*.avif  binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.eot   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.mp4   binary
*.webm  binary

# The current schema, one file per object, generated from supabase/migrations/ by
# `npm run db -- generate` and checked by CI. Nobody edits it by hand, so collapse it
# in diffs (linguist-generated). Matters most on dev -> main release PRs, where it
# would otherwise add thousands of lines a reviewer has to scroll past to reach the
# real changes.
#
# Also exempt from eol normalization (-text): the long-description CHECK names a
# literal carriage return in its btrim set, and pg_dump writes that byte into the
# string literal in one of these files. Normalizing CRLF->LF on commit deletes the CR
# from inside the SQL string, so the committed files can never match a fresh dump —
# CI's comparison would then fail on every branch with nothing anyone could do about
# it. These files are machine-written and never hand-edited, so storing them
# byte-exact costs nothing.
supabase/schema/** -text linguist-generated=true

# The two baseline migrations, which are the squashed numbered history: a
# pg_dump of the public schema, and the reference data that went with it. Both
# are machine-written and never hand-edited, and between them they are about
# 91,000 lines — so, like the schema above, collapse them in diffs, or every
# dev -> main release PR until they are behind `main` buries its real changes
# under them.
#
# Only the schema half is exempt from eol normalization (-text), for the reason
# the schema entry gives: it holds the same carriage return inside the same
# CHECK constraint's string literal, and normalizing it away would give every
# fresh database a constraint one byte different from the one production runs.
supabase/migrations/00266_baseline_schema.sql -text linguist-generated=true
supabase/migrations/00267_baseline_reference_data.sql linguist-generated=true
